WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.980
- Hey, guys, welcome to another episode

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.560
of the Wednesday Checkup.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.160
Today, I wanna tell you
about a really interesting

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.440
patient encounter I had just the other day

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.220
and then we're gonna follow up that story

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.770
with one of my favorite
segments, Mail Time.

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:13.626
You ready?

00:00:13.626 --> 00:00:14.459
(whooping)

00:00:14.459 --> 00:00:17.042
(mellow music)

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.070
My nurse rooms the patient,
takes all the vitals,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.150
brings all the information,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.880
including the chief complaint to me.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.710
Tells me that the patient
has normal vital signs,

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.840
normal blood pressure, pulse, high 90s.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.130
That he's a mid 60-year-old male.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.800
That the breathing rate is normal.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.390
And that the patient's chief complaint

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:40.110
is that there's this redness
surrounding the right foot,

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.770
that the patient is worried about,

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.690
that the antibiotics haven't really,

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:44.870
that they haven't really seen

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.950
an improvement in the
redness of the right foot,

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.460
despite using antibiotics
in the last 48 hours.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.450
Prior to walking into the
room with the patient,

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.350
I did look at the electronic health record

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:54.183
and found out they have

00:00:54.183 --> 00:00:56.580
an extensive list of medical conditions

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.610
and are take a lot of
medications for them.

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.200
Hypothyroid, osteoporosis,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.670
diabetes type 2, high
cholesterol, atrial fibrillation,

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:07.730
so I know that this patient
does have medications onboard

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.460
and other conditions at play.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.080
It's important for me as a doctor to check

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.610
that before going into a room

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.570
so that I can see how
maybe those illnesses

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.340
and those medications can be factoring

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.510
in to the current problem.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.200
After hearing the story about the foot,

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.810
I asked the patient if there
were any other complaints.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.840
And I generally have a list of

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.670
what we call a review of systems,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.410
where I ask a few general questions

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:31.310
just to see how the
patient's doing overall.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:34.700
Constitutional symptoms
like fever, fatigue,

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.460
unexplained weight loss,

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.030
then we have the cardiovascular ones,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.030
palpitations, chest pain,

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.220
then with the respiratory
ones, shortness of breath.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:43.460
You sort of get the idea.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:44.950
The patient did mention that he felt

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.410
a little bit more short
of breath than usual.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.270
He attributed that to the pain

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:51.760
and discomfort from his right leg.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:53.810
That he thought maybe he
wasn't sleeping as well

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.180
and there were all these
sorts of explanations

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.040
he had in his mind for
why that was happening.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.990
So I listened to the patient's lungs,

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:01.510
crystal clear, no problem.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.480
When I listened to the patient's heart,

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.980
I hear something that I
didn't expect to hear.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:09.660
The patient was having an
irregularly irregular rhythm,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.680
meaning that it was an irregular rate,

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.100
that is was above 100,

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.590
and it was an irregular rhythm,

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:16.880
meaning that it didn't have
a consistent beat to it.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.410
Ba-boom, boom, ba-boo-boo-boom, boom.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.080
That is essentially the
beat that we would hear

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.470
if a patient has atrial fibrillation.

00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:27.050
Now I know this patient
has a history of that

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.530
so it makes it a little
bit more easy for me

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.240
to expect to hear that.

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.160
But a patient who has atrial
fibrillation actively,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.470
at a fast rate is dangerous.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.300
Reason being is the heart
doesn't like to be beating

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.900
very rapidly for a long period of time.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.470
At rest, your heart rate should be

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.070
somewhere between 60 and 100.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.450
However, my patient was beating
somewhere in the low 100s

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:51.460
when I listened and I checked their pulse.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:52.307
I asked the patient,

00:02:52.307 --> 00:02:54.950
"Have you felt any palpitations
in the last few days?"

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.340
Meaning that they feel
like their heart's racing

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.830
or beating outside of their chest.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.640
That's a very common way to explain it.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.887
And the patient says to me,

00:03:01.887 --> 00:03:03.367
"You know doctor, the last few days,

00:03:03.367 --> 00:03:04.677
"I did feel a little bit

00:03:04.677 --> 00:03:06.870
"and I wasn't sure if it's my AFib,"

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.067
the atrial fibrillation, "kicking in.

00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:11.567
"But it went away, so I
assumed nothing of it.

00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:12.460
"Is that okay?"

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:14.550
Let me do something, let me get an EKG

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.370
just to see how your heart is doing,

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:17.790
how fast is it beating,

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:20.410
confirm that this is fact AFib,

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:21.930
and we'll move on from there.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.500
And we sort of put the
foot issue to the side

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.860
because A, he's actually
being treated for it,

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.930
and B, the more concerning
issue for the time being

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:30.800
would be the heart.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.860
Upon getting the EKG, we found out

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:34.380
that my physical exam was correct

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.560
and the patient was having AFib.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.740
And he was having AFib with RVR.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.010
I know that's a mouthful.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:42.590
AFib is that atrial fibrillation

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:43.870
where the top of the heart beats

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.290
irregularly at an irregular rate.

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.010
But then his ventricles,

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.120
as a result of having those excess beats,

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.890
was also beating very quickly.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:53.490
That's the lower portion of the heart

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:55.810
that actually pumps out blood
to the rest of the body.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:58.300
His heart was working very hard

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.780
in order to maintain this rhythm.

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:00.700
But this isn't normal.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:02.240
My patient's at rest, his heart

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.590
shouldn't be beating that quickly.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:06.320
Upon further review of that EKG,

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:08.280
I found that he was having segments

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.730
of what's known as ST depressions.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:14.680
And what ST depressions,
specifically on an EKG signify

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.650
is that the heart isn't
getting enough blood

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:18.483
and it's suffering.

00:04:18.483 --> 00:04:20.030
It's actually being choked out.

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:23.900
We call this a type 2 MI which
ones a type 2 heart attack.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:25.650
When the heart is beating so fast

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.100
that it's not getting enough blood

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.270
because it's being overworked

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.860
and there's actual damage to
the muscle tissue of the heart.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.247
So I told him that we have to call 911

00:04:33.247 --> 00:04:35.760
and we have to get him
sent over to the hospital

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.790
in order to contain this rate,

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:39.310
slow the rate down,

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:42.030
and then figure out what our
long-term plan is gonna be.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.430
Now this was very stressful

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.100
because the patient
started getting emotional,

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.040
they started getting worried and upset

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.870
because they thought they
were coming in for their foot,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.180
but here we are, diagnosing
them with a heart attack.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.530
My patient ended up going
to the emergency room.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.440
They gave him IV medications.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.600
They made sure that the rate subsided,

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.610
that the enzymes that were
leaking from the damaged heart

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.960
started going down and
the heart was improving.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.530
And once that started happening,

00:05:05.530 --> 00:05:07.770
they had a cardiovascular
doctor see the patient

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.330
and decide what the plan
wanna gonna be moving forward.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.210
And part of that was to increase

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:13.870
the dosage of one medication

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.530
and decrease the dosage of another.

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.410
And then have them follow up in one week

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:18.670
with that same cardiologist.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:20.060
The point of this being

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.760
is that as a doctor, you
never know what do expect,

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.590
especially at a family medicine office.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.510
I had a patient on my
schedule for an infected foot,

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.840
and here I am, diagnosing
him with a heart attack

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:31.470
that they were walking around with.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:33.970
They actually walked a
few blocks to my office.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:36.310
A mistake I see a lot
of young doctors make

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.780
is to get pigeonholed into a diagnosis.

00:05:38.780 --> 00:05:42.020
They see a red, swollen
foot on their screen.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.750
They only look at that body part

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.120
and they say, "Okay, well
here's a diagnosis for this,"

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.850
and they forget that
there's a whole person

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:48.920
sitting in front of them.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:50.820
It's important to take that whole person

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:53.200
to consideration when treating a patient.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.980
Yes, the complaint is
about an infected foot,

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.850
but the real concern was
with my patient's heart

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.610
and the only way I could find that out

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.980
is through a thorough
history and physical exam.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.610
Had I not done those things,

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.107
just looked at the foot, said,
"Okay, the foot's improving,

00:06:09.107 --> 00:06:10.407
"you know, keep taking your antibiotics,

00:06:10.407 --> 00:06:11.700
"let's have you follow up."

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:13.930
I would have been doing a
huge disservice to my patient.

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:16.360
We have to treat the
human sitting in front us

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.770
and not the ailment,

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:19.870
or not the complaint that
they're bringing to us.

00:06:19.870 --> 00:06:22.690
The more you can do that
as a doctor, as a person,

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:25.430
as a police officer, it
really doesn't even matter,

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:26.670
the better you're gonna fair

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:27.700
and the better the person sitting

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:28.750
in front of you is gonna fair.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:30.950
Let's have some fun and open some packages

00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:34.670
via Mail Time, courtesy of my mail bag.

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:38.377
West Orange, New Jersey,
that's not too far.

00:06:38.377 --> 00:06:39.820
(bag crinkling)

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:41.270
Oh, these are gonna be cuff links

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.070
because I can tell from the cuff link box.

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:44.270
Ooh.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:45.280
These are

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.340
dope.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.285
These are little stethoscopes.

00:06:48.285 --> 00:06:51.120
Ooh, look at this little checkerboard tie.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.770
If it matches, you gotta wear it.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:54.730
Whew.

00:06:54.730 --> 00:06:55.563
Easy work.

00:06:55.563 --> 00:06:56.760
Thank you for your wonderful gift.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:57.920
I think I look pretty fly

00:06:57.920 --> 00:06:59.840
and I can't wait to wear these cuff links.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.803
Fort Walton Beach, Florida.

00:07:01.803 --> 00:07:02.994
(gasping)

00:07:02.994 --> 00:07:04.452
Snickers!

00:07:04.452 --> 00:07:05.285
(gasps)

00:07:05.285 --> 00:07:06.950
Did you get Bear his favorite toy?

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:10.460
No joke, this is Bear's favorite toy.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.980
Little squirrels that you put in here

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.260
and then he has to get them out.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.030
Bear, I have a gift for you.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:17.000
Oh, they make noise.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.340
This is legit, Bear's favorite toy.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:19.410
He's gonna lose his mind.

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:20.670
Oh, it's a mug.

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:23.170
Aw, me and Roxy sitting side by side.

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:24.210
How did you do this?

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.680
Husky Dad, me and Roxy hanging out.

00:07:26.680 --> 00:07:27.750
Thank you, family.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:30.420
Okay, this one's from Texas.

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:31.280
Whoop!

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.040
Whoa.

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:34.920
Swarovski pens,

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.700
with my name on them.

00:07:36.700 --> 00:07:38.110
But the only thing is,

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:39.060
I'm not a DO

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:41.300
and MD,

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:42.400
I'm just a DO.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:43.800
Problematic?

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:44.910
This is from Hungary

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:47.230
and I've been to Hungary many of times.

00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:48.730
Not many of times, I've been there twice.

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:50.390
I imagine this is gonna be a drawing

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:52.440
'cause it's with some
really heavy cardboard.

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.273
Here, let's see.

00:07:53.273 --> 00:07:55.317
"Dear Mike, I couldn't
decide, so I did both.

00:07:55.317 --> 00:07:57.220
"Hope you like it, best regards."

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:58.053
What?

00:07:58.053 --> 00:08:00.110
This is by far the best drawing

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:02.260
I've ever seen of me in my life.

00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:04.140
This is pencil, folks.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.713
Is this ridiculous or what?

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.770
- Wow.
- Dude, these are amazing.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.090
I'm gonna have these framed.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:13.606
I don't know where I'm gonna put them

00:08:13.606 --> 00:08:14.439
because it's kind of awkward

00:08:14.439 --> 00:08:16.660
hanging pictures of
yourself in your own house,

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:17.880
but these are amazing.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:19.140
Like, this looks like me.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.320
Beau Smith or Bea Smith,

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:23.260
please, send me your Instagram.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:25.010
I wanna put this on my story

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:26.250
and share this with the world.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:27.520
Your art is amazing.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:30.070
I mean, like you can't
draw better then that.

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:31.170
This is from Ohio.

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:32.003
Okay.

00:08:32.003 --> 00:08:34.630
Okay, here we go, we got socks.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:35.590
Get outta here!

00:08:35.590 --> 00:08:38.680
Socks with Roxy and Bear's face on them.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:40.280
Come on now, chum on.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.210
I'm gonna wear these to work

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:43.270
and people are gonna judge me

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:44.760
and I don't even care at all.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.210
Thank you, Kenyatta,
very much appreciated.

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:48.467
Stay being an awesome nurse.

00:08:48.467 --> 00:08:51.237
"I've sent you a T-shirt I
make at our small family farm.

00:08:51.237 --> 00:08:52.847
"I guessed you wear a large or extra large

00:08:52.847 --> 00:08:54.097
"so whichever one doesn't fit,

00:08:54.097 --> 00:08:55.960
"feel free to do whatever
you want with it."

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:58.400
Okay, this is a very interesting shirt.

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:00.650
Support your local beekeeper,
I'm down with that,

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:02.393
but why is the bee throwing up?

00:09:02.393 --> 00:09:03.226
(retches)

00:09:03.226 --> 00:09:05.400
Fun fact, all my videos are now captioned

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.140
in English and Spanish,

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:08.840
so click here to check this one out

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:09.890
and have good laugh.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.643
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:09:12.643 --> 00:09:15.226
(cheery music)

